# Core envelope verification

[Run the 1.0.0 release notebook in Livebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fv1.0.0%2Fdocs%2Flivebooks%2Fbap-walkthrough.livemd).

Produce a contract-major 1 grant and holder proof, verify the envelope, and refuse changed bytes, arguments, and stale proofs.

Run every cell in order. Keys are generated only in memory; persisted outputs contain no keys,
signatures, credentials, or nonces. Verification leaves trust and execution policy with the host.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.0.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

The cell returns `:ok`.

## Ephemeral keys

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  {issuer_public, issuer_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
  {holder_public, holder_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)

  {:ok, holder_thumbprint} =
    BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Jwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(holder_public, %{})

  {byte_size(issuer_public), byte_size(holder_thumbprint)}
)
true = notebook_value === (
  {32, 32}
)
notebook_value
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{32, 32}
```

The cell returns `{32, 32}`: a raw 32-byte Ed25519 public key, and the raw 32-byte RFC 7638
thumbprint of the holder key that the grant will name.

## Build and sign a grant

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Grant, Operation}

  grant = %Grant{
    key_id: "issuer-1",
    issuer: "https://issuer.example",
    grant_id: "grant-2026-001",
    audiences: ["https://service.example"],
    issued_at: 1_800_000_000,
    not_before: 1_800_000_000,
    expires_at: 1_800_003_600,
    holder_thumbprint: holder_thumbprint,
    operations: [
      %Operation{
        name: "read_record",
        selectors: [
          {:equals, ["record", "region"], {:string, "us-east"}},
          {:one_of, ["record", "tier"], [{:string, "gold"}, {:string, "platinum"}]},
          :all
        ]
      }
    ]
  }

  {:ok, signing_input} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.grant_signing_input(grant, %{})
  issuer_signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, signing_input.message, [issuer_private, :ed25519])
  {:ok, grant_compact} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.assemble_compact(signing_input, issuer_signature)

  grant_compact
)
true = match?("eyJ" <> _, notebook_value)
:compact_assembled
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:compact_assembled
```

The generated compact begins with its encoded protected header: a compact JWS whose first segment is the base64url form of
the protected header `{"alg":"EdDSA",…}`. The signature is fresh on every run.

## Build and sign the holder proof

The proof binds this grant (through `ath`, the digest of the exact grant compact bytes), this
request (through the server-derived operation and typed arguments), and this context.

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Proof

  now = 1_800_001_000

  proof = %Proof{
    holder_public_key: holder_public,
    proof_id: "proof-2026-001",
    method: "GET",
    target_uri: "https://service.example/records/1",
    issued_at: now,
    nonce: nil,
    invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120002",
    operation: "read_record",
    grant_compact: grant_compact,
    cast_arguments:
      {:object,
       [
         {"record", {:object, [{"tier", {:string, "gold"}}, {"region", {:string, "us-east"}}]}}
       ]}
  }

  {:ok, proof_input} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.proof_signing_input(proof, %{})
  holder_signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, proof_input.message, [holder_private, :ed25519])
  {:ok, proof_compact} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.assemble_compact(proof_input, holder_signature)

  proof_compact
)
true = match?("eyJ" <> _, notebook_value)
:compact_assembled
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:compact_assembled
```

The generated compact begins with its encoded protected header: the proof is a compact JWS of type `dpop+jwt`.

## Verify the grant

The verifier holds no state and reads no clock. The caller supplies the trusted key, the
expected context, and the evaluation time.

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{TrustedIssuer, ExpectedGrant}

  trusted = %TrustedIssuer{key_id: "issuer-1", public_key: issuer_public}

  expected_grant = %ExpectedGrant{
    issuer: "https://issuer.example",
    audience: "https://service.example",
    evaluation_time: now,
    clock_skew: 60,
    bounds: %{}
  }

  {:ok, grant_facts} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.verify_grant(grant_compact, trusted, expected_grant)

  Map.take(Map.from_struct(grant_facts), [:issuer, :grant_id, :authorization])
)
true = notebook_value === (
  %{issuer: "https://issuer.example", grant_id: "grant-2026-001", authorization: :not_evaluated}
)
notebook_value
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{issuer: "https://issuer.example", grant_id: "grant-2026-001", authorization: :not_evaluated}
```

The cell returns `%{issuer: "https://issuer.example", grant_id: "grant-2026-001", authorization: :not_evaluated}`.
Facts carry identifiers, digests, and times, never keys or signatures, and
`authorization: :not_evaluated` says that deciding is still your job.

## Verify the full envelope

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Credentials, ExpectedRequest}

  credentials = %Credentials{grant: grant_compact, proof: proof_compact}

  expected_request = %ExpectedRequest{
    trusted_issuer: trusted,
    issuer: "https://issuer.example",
    audience: "https://service.example",
    method: "GET",
    target_uri: "https://service.example/records/1",
    invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120002",
    operation: "read_record",
    cast_arguments:
      {:object,
       [
         {"record", {:object, [{"tier", {:string, "gold"}}, {"region", {:string, "us-east"}}]}}
       ]},
    evaluation_time: now,
    clock_skew: 60,
    proof_max_age: 300,
    nonce: :not_required,
    bounds: %{}
  }

  {:ok, envelope_facts} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(credentials, expected_request)

  Map.take(Map.from_struct(envelope_facts), [:operation, :authorization])
)
true = notebook_value === (
  %{operation: "read_record", authorization: :not_evaluated}
)
notebook_value
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{operation: "read_record", authorization: :not_evaluated}
```

The cell returns `%{operation: "read_record", authorization: :not_evaluated}`. The facts struct
has redacted ordinary inspection. Never serialize it or log raw request bindings; the
explicit projection above shows only the operation and the non-authorization marker.

## Every attack fails closed

```elixir
notebook_value = (
  # A changed byte in the grant signature:
  tampered =
    grant_compact
    |> String.split(".")
    |> List.update_at(2, fn segment ->
      i = div(String.length(segment), 2)
      <<head::binary-size(^i), byte, tail::binary>> = segment
      head <> <<Bitwise.bxor(byte, 1)>> <> tail
    end)
    |> Enum.join(".")

  # The same proof against different arguments (the tier selector disallows "bronze"):
  wrong_args = %ExpectedRequest{
    expected_request
    | cast_arguments:
        {:object,
         [
           {"record", {:object, [{"tier", {:string, "bronze"}}, {"region", {:string, "us-east"}}]}}
         ]}
  }

  # A stale proof, outside the maximum age plus the clock skew:
  stale = %ExpectedRequest{expected_request | evaluation_time: now + 361}

  %{
    tampered_grant:
      BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(
        %Credentials{grant: tampered, proof: proof_compact},
        expected_request
      ),
    selector_disallowed: BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(credentials, wrong_args),
    stale_proof: BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(credentials, stale)
  }
)
true = notebook_value === (
  %{tampered_grant: {:error, :invalid}, selector_disallowed: {:error, :invalid}, stale_proof: {:error, :invalid}}
)
notebook_value
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{tampered_grant: {:error, :invalid}, selector_disallowed: {:error, :invalid}, stale_proof: {:error, :invalid}}
```

The cell returns `%{tampered_grant: {:error, :invalid}, selector_disallowed: {:error, :invalid}, stale_proof: {:error, :invalid}}`.
Each attack gets exactly `{:error, :invalid}`: no partial information and no exception. The error
never says which check failed; the
[troubleshooting guide](https://hexdocs.pm/bounded_authority_protocol/troubleshooting.html)
walks through diagnosing a rejection from your own inputs.
